<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Maurice, Elector of Saxony</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1521">1521</a>–<a href="/years/1553">1553</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Moritz of Saxony</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Maurice was Duke and then Elector of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albertine-line">Albertine line</a>. Though Lutheran, he sided with Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schmalkaldic-war">Schmalkaldic War</a> and in 1547 received the electoral title taken from his Ernestine cousin. In 1552 he turned against Charles, forcing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-passau">Peace of Passau</a>, and he was killed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sievershausen">Battle of Sievershausen</a> in 1553.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maurice-elector-of-saxony.jpg" alt="Maurice, Elector of Saxony" width="320" height="429" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lucas Cranach the Younger · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Moritz_Sachsen.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Maurice was the fourth child but first son of the future Henry IV, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duke-of-saxony">Duke of Saxony</a>, and his Protestant wife, Catherine of Mecklenburg-Schwerin. His uncle Duke George had him educated as a Catholic, but in 1536 his father became a Protestant and, on succeeding George in 1539, made the duchy Protestant. In 1539 Maurice went to live at Torgau with his cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-frederick-i-elector-of-saxony">John Frederick I, Elector of Saxony</a>, whom he despised. With another cousin, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-i-landgrave-of-hesse">Philip I, Landgrave of Hesse</a>, he formed a lifelong friendship. On 9 January 1541 he married Philip's eldest daughter, Agnes, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marburg">Marburg</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Maurice succeeded his father on 18 August 1541. He joined the imperial army in campaigns in 1542, 1543 and 1544, while confiscating Church property at home. From the wealth of dissolved monasteries he founded the princes' schools at Schulpforta, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meissen">Meissen</a> and Grimma. He refused to join the Protestant Schmalkaldic League, largely, it is generally held, from hatred of John Frederick and the emperor's promise of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> electorate.</p><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> placed John Frederick under the Imperial Ban in 1546, Maurice undertook to enforce it. He occupied the electorate almost without a fight, and at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-muhlberg">Battle of Mühlberg</a> on 24 April 1547 the imperial forces captured both John Frederick and Philip of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a>. By the Capitulation of Wittenberg John Frederick ceded the electorate and large lands, and on 4 June 1547 Maurice was raised to elector in a brief ceremony in the field camp. Philip, to whom he had promised that he would not be imprisoned, was taken prisoner, and Maurice was called a Judas by his compatriots. He was formally invested at the Diet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a> on 25 February 1548.</p><p>Angered by Charles's plans to restore <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> and by Philip's continued imprisonment, Maurice used a commission to subdue <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a> in 1550 to raise an army. In January 1552 he agreed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-chambord">Treaty of Chambord</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-of-france">Henry II of France</a>, who then took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toul">Toul</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verdun">Verdun</a> and Cambrai. In March the rebels overran southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and forced Charles to flee over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> and free Philip. Maurice then negotiated with Ferdinand I, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-passau">Peace of Passau</a> of August 1552 provisionally guaranteed the Lutheran position.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Maurice then sided again with Ferdinand and other princes against Albert Alcibiades of Brandenburg-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kulmbach">Kulmbach</a>, who had rejected the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/passau">Passau</a> armistice. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sievershausen">Battle of Sievershausen</a> on 9 July 1553 Maurice won, but was shot in the stomach from behind and died two days later in the field camp, aged 32. With no surviving male heir, he was succeeded as elector by his brother Augustus.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Maurice, Elector of Saxony” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Maurice of Saxony</b>, who had sided with the emperor and received the electorate, turned against him in the Princes’ Revolt of 1552, forcing the Peace of Passau, and died in 1553 from wounds received at the Battle of Sievershausen.</blockquote><a href="/empire/electorate-of-saxony">Electorate of Saxony · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freiberg">Freiberg</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in Sievershausen, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.91,13.34&amp;year=1541" aria-label="Freiberg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4853 398 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Maurice, Elector of Saxony"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5303 668h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5303 668h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.91,13.34&amp;year=1541">What was here in 1541? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
